use std
into draco_check.amni

Imports: os, gfx.draco and gfx.gltf. A standalone program that checks the Draco decoder on the CPU and prints one line per check, then "draco: ok" when every check passed or "draco: FAILED" otherwise. The decoder already exists and must not be defined here: `draco::decode(src)` takes a read-only byte slice and gives a bool; after it, `draco::points` (int), `draco::faces` (u32 list, three point numbers per triangle), and the int lists `draco::att_uid`, `draco::att_dtype`, `draco::att_comps` and `draco::att_first` describe the result, `draco::values` (real list) holds every attribute's values point by point starting at its `att_first`, and `draco::error()` gives the reason for the last failure as text. A module bool `failed` starts false; `check(name, ok, detail)` prints "PASS " or "FAIL " then the name, then " (" the detail ")" when the detail is not empty, and sets `failed` when ok is false.

Time is always read as `os::clock_ns()` with the namespace written out, because a bare clock_ns is ambiguous here. `u32at(b, at)` reads a 32-bit little-endian unsigned number from a byte slice. `expected(b, at, dtype)` reads one value of the given Draco data type at byte offset at as a real: 1 signed byte, 2 unsigned byte, 3 signed 16-bit, 4 unsigned 16-bit, 5 signed 32-bit, 6 unsigned 32-bit, 9 a 32-bit IEEE float, all little-endian; `dsize(dtype)` is 1, 1, 2, 2, 4, 4 for types 1 to 6 and 4 for 9.

The cases file is the first program argument when one is given (`os::arg_count()` above 1, read with `os::arg(1, out)`), otherwise "hc/test/gfx/fixtures/draco_cases.bin". When `os::read_file` fails it checks "cases file" as false with the path as detail and stops. The file is a list of records, each: a name (32-bit length then bytes), the Draco stream (32-bit length then bytes), the point count and the face count (32-bit each), three 32-bit point numbers per face, the attribute count, then per attribute its unique id, data type and component count (32-bit each) and its values (32-bit byte length then the bytes: point by point, each point's components in the data type). Records are read until the end of the file into lists of offsets; a record that runs past the end fails the check "cases file is whole". The stream's kind comes from its header: byte 8 is 0 for sequential and 1 for edgebreaker; the flags are the 16-bit number at byte 9 and metadata is present when they have bit 0x8000; for an edgebreaker stream without metadata, byte 11 is 0 for standard and 2 for valence.

`same(rec)` decodes the record's stream and gives true only when the call gives true, `draco::points` equals the point count, `draco::faces` has 3 times the face count entries equal one for one to the expected point numbers, and for every expected attribute there is a decoded attribute with that unique id whose data type and component count match and whose values (point count times component count of them from its `att_first`) equal the expected values exactly. It also fills a module text `last` with the first difference it found (the name, then what differed: "decode: " and `draco::error()`, "points", "faces", or "attribute " and the id).

D1: every record decodes the same. Count the records that pass and the total; the detail is the passed count, " of ", the total and, when one fails, `last` for the first failing record. Also add up the faces and the time spent inside the decode calls (from `os::clock_ns()`).

D2: the same records split by kind each pass: sequential, edgebreaker standard, edgebreaker valence, and streams with metadata, plus records whose name contains " seq ", " f0 ", " f3 ", " f5 ", " f10 ", " g0 ", " g7 " or " g8 " (the encoder build and speed). One check line per kind with its passed and total counts; a kind with no records fails.

D3: damaged streams never crash and never give a broken result. For every record decode: the stream cut short by one byte, the stream cut to half its length, the stream with its first byte changed to 0x55, a copy with the middle byte bitwise-xored with 0x5a, and a copy with every byte from the middle on set to 0xff. After each call that gives false, `draco::faces`, `draco::values` and `draco::att_uid` must be empty; after each call that gives true, every face entry must be below `draco::points` and the values list must hold exactly `draco::points` times the sum of the component counts. The bad-header calls must give false. The detail is the number of damaged decodes tried.

D4: big streams decode fast. Three files in the same folder as the cases file: "draco_big_s0.drc" (valence), "draco_big_s7.drc" (standard) and "draco_big_s10.drc" (standard, no prediction), each the same 220 by 110 sphere. Each is decoded 7 times, timing every decode with `os::clock_ns()`; one check line per file whose detail is the face count, the fastest decode in milliseconds with one decimal and the speed in millions of triangles per second with one decimal; it passes when the decode gives true, the face count is the same for all three and the speed is above 2 million triangles per second.

D5: whole files load the same. These already exist in namespace gltf and must not be defined here: `gltf::reset()` empties the loader; `gltf::load(path)` reads a .glb and gives an asset number, or -1 with the reason from `gltf::error()`; the module lists `gltf::verts` (f32) and `gltf::indices` (u32) and `gltf::prims` (records) are the loaded data. A helper `snapshot(path)` resets the loader, loads the file and gives a copy of `gltf::verts`, a copy of `gltf::indices` and the number of prims, or reports the load error. "scene_draco.glb" (a primitive compressed with KHR_draco_mesh_compression) is compared against "scene_draco_ref.glb" (the same scene with that primitive stored as the Draco reference decoder's output) from the cases file's folder: one check line passing when both load, the prim counts match and the vertex and index lists are equal value for value. The detail is the vertex float count, the index count and the prim count.

`main` runs D1, D2 and D3 when the cases file loads, then D4 and D5 always, and finally prints "draco: ok" or "draco: FAILED".
